<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Judas Maccabeus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 160 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Judah Maccabee · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasmonean-dynasty">Hasmonean dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Judas Maccabeus was a Jewish priest and military leader, the third son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mattathias">Mattathias</a>, who led the revolt against the Seleucid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iv-epiphanes">Antiochus IV</a> from 166 BCE. He recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and rededicated the Temple in 164 BCE, an event commemorated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanukkah">Hanukkah</a>. He was killed at the Battle of Elasa in 160 BCE, and his brothers Jonathan and Simon continued the struggle.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/judas-maccabeus.jpg" alt="Judas Maccabeus" width="177" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Paul Rubens and workshop · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter_Paul_Rubens_and_workshop_-_The_Triumph_of_Judas_Maccabeus_(Bust_of_Judas_Maccabeus).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> was the third son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mattathias">Mattathias</a>, a priest from the village of Modi'in. In 167 BCE Mattathias and his sons Judah, Eleazar, Simon, John and Jonathan began a revolt against the Seleucid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iv-epiphanes">Antiochus IV Epiphanes</a>, who had issued decrees forbidding Jewish religious practices. After Mattathias died in 166 BCE, Judah took over the leadership in accordance with his father's deathbed wish, and the First Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maccabees">Maccabees</a> praises his valour and military talent.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> received the surname Maccabee in the early days of the revolt. Its origin is unknown. One suggestion derives it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> maqqaba, a hammer, in recognition of his ferocity in battle. The name came to signify all the Hasmoneans who fought in the revolt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Facing superior Seleucid forces, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> avoided their regular army at first and used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare. He defeated a small force under Apollonius, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, at Nahal el-Haramiah, killed Apollonius and kept his sword until his death. He then routed a larger army under Seron near Beth-Horon and, at the Battle of Emmaus, defeated the forces of Nicanor and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgias">Gorgias</a>, sent by the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysias">Lysias</a>, after a forced night march that eluded Gorgias.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> drove his enemies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> except for the garrison in the citadel of Acra, purified the Temple and restored its service on 25 Kislev, which is 14 December 164 BCE. The reconsecration became the holiday of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanukkah">Hanukkah</a>. At Beth-zechariah the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> won their first major victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maccabees">Maccabees</a>, but a peace at the end of 163 BCE restored religious freedom and the Temple to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. In 161 BCE his army destroyed a Seleucid force near Adasa and killed Nicanor, and Judah made an agreement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>, the first recorded contract between the Jewish people and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> did not change the policy of King Demetrius I Soter, who sent a new army under Bacchides. Of its 20,000 men, so many outnumbered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>'s force that most of his men left the field and urged him to do the same, but he stood his ground. At the Battle of Elasa he and those who stayed with him were killed. His brothers took his body and buried it in the family tomb at Modiin.</p><p>His death stirred the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to renewed resistance, and under his brothers Jonathan and Simon the Jews eventually won independence and the freedom to worship. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> inspired writers, painters and composers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-frideric-handel">George Frideric Handel</a> wrote the oratorio Judas Maccabaeus in 1746.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Judas Maccabeus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hanukkah, the eight-day festival of Judaism that begins on 25 Kislev, usually in December, commemorates an event: the rededication of the Temple in Jerusalem in 164 BCE by <b>Judas Maccabeus</b> and his followers, after the Seleucid king Antiochus IV Epiphanes had desecrated it.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Judas</b> recaptured the Temple in 164 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/hasmonean-kingdom">Hasmonean Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Elasa. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
